@typeparam TItem
@typeparam TValue

@* A dropdown. A plain HTML <select>, and that is the whole point of it.

   Every dropdown in this admin used to be a TelerikDropDownList, and on an Android phone none of
   them worked. Kendo measures a popup before it shows it — it drops the list into the page at
   `left: -10000px`, reads its size, then moves it under its field. That measuring element is an
   ordinary absolutely-positioned div, so for one frame the document is ten thousand pixels wide,
   and Chrome on Android answers a document that wide by zooming the page out to its minimum
   scale. On a 360px phone the layout viewport becomes 1440×2960: the desktop breakpoints light
   up, the dialog re-centres itself in a viewport four times the screen and lands off it, and all
   that is left is the modal's grey overlay — which is what any tap then lands on, closing the
   dropdown. iPhone never did this, which is why it looked like a Galaxy bug rather than a
   Kendo one.

   Pinning the viewport's minimum-scale fixed it in Chrome's Android emulator and not on the real
   phone, so the popup is gone instead. A <select> has no popup to position: the list is drawn by
   the operating system, outside the page, and cannot move the document or the layout. It is also
   the control a phone already knows how to show — a full-height native wheel with big touch
   targets — which is better on a phone than anything drawn in the page.

   The station has been doing exactly this since it was written (see AxisField). This is that
   decision applied everywhere else.

   WHAT IS GIVEN UP: the filter box Kendo put at the top of a long list. Nothing else — no styling
   (`.field` is the same class every text box wears), no keyboard behaviour (a <select> types
   ahead natively), no placeholder.

   Values travel as strings, because that is the only thing an <option> can hold. TextOf and
   ValueOf are passed as functions rather than as Kendo's "TextField"/"ValueField" strings, so a
   renamed DTO property breaks the build instead of quietly emptying a list at runtime. *@

<select class="field @Class" value="@Key(Value)" @onchange="Pick">

    @* The placeholder, and it is SELECTABLE — picking it is how a filter is cleared, which is what
       Kendo's DefaultText did too.

       `selected` is decided by "does anything in the list match the value held", not by comparing
       against a blank. That one test covers all four ways of holding nothing that the call sites
       actually use — null, "", 0, and a stale id whose option is no longer in the list — instead
       of each of them needing to be spelled out here. *@
    @if (Placeholder is not null)
    {
        <option value="" selected="@(!HasMatch)">@Placeholder</option>
    }

    @foreach (var item in Data)
    {
        var key = Key(ValueOf(item));
        <option value="@key" selected="@(key == Key(Value))">@TextOf(item)</option>
    }
</select>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IEnumerable<TItem> Data { get; set; } = Array.Empty<TItem>();

    /// <summary>What the reader sees on a row.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public Func<TItem, string> TextOf { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>What picking that row is worth.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public Func<TItem, TValue?> ValueOf { get; set; } = default!;

    [Parameter] public TValue? Value { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<TValue?> ValueChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The first entry, shown while nothing is chosen. Omit it and the list has no way back to empty.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Placeholder { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    static string Key(TValue? value) => value?.ToString() ?? "";

    bool HasMatch => Data.Any(item => Key(ValueOf(item)) == Key(Value));

    /// <summary>
    /// The chosen row, found by matching the option's string back against the list rather than by
    /// converting it to TValue. TValue is an int here and a string there, and a generic parse would
    /// be a pile of type tests to arrive at a value the list is already holding.
    /// </summary>
    async Task Pick(ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        var raw = e.Value?.ToString() ?? "";

        foreach (var item in Data)
        {
            if (Key(ValueOf(item)) != raw) continue;
            await ValueChanged.InvokeAsync(ValueOf(item));
            return;
        }

        // The placeholder, or an option that has since left the list. Either way: nothing is held.
        await ValueChanged.InvokeAsync(default);
    }
}
